## Inference from Overloaded Function Types in TypeScript

When a conditional/utility type infers from an overloaded function type (a type with multiple call signatures), TypeScript uses the **last** overload signature for inference.

### Example

```typescript
type Overloaded = {
  (a: string): number;   // 1st overload
  (a: number): string;   // 2nd overload (last)
};

type InferParam<T> = T extends (a: infer P) => any ? P : never;

type Result = InferParam<Overloaded>; // number (from the LAST signature)
```

### Why the Last Overload?

TypeScript internally represents an overloaded function type as an **intersection** of individual function types:

```typescript
type Overloaded = ((a: string) => number) & ((a: number) => string);
```

When a conditional type (`T extends (a: infer P) => any ? ...`) performs inference against this intersection, TypeScript resolves to the **last** constituent signature. This is a known and documented behavior (see [TypeScript issue #26349](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript/issues/26349) and related discussions).

### Workaround

If you need to target a specific overload, you can extract it explicitly:

```typescript
type FirstOverload<T> = T extends { (a: infer P): infer R } ? (a: P) => R : never;
// Still picks last... so you'd need to manually pick:

type PickOverload<T, N extends number> = /* manual indexing via tuple tricks */;
```

Or simply define the specific signature you want to infer from:

```typescript
type Specific = (a: string) => number;
type Result2 = InferParam<Specific>; // string ✓
```

### Key Takeaway

> **The last overload position is used for inference.** This applies to all conditional types, `infer` expressions, and utility types (like `Parameters<>`, `ReturnType<>`) that operate on overloaded function types.

\boxed{last}

